VERDICT
======================================================================================================
  THE POSITIVE CONTROL PASSED, which is the only reason to trust the rest: check E
  rediscovered the twoloop_laneC_a0 -> graviton supersession from file dates and filename
  keywords alone, with no knowledge of the physics and without being told to look.

  WHAT EACH CHECK IS FOR, and what it is NOT:
   A  STANDING open-claims with an uncited, newer, closing script on overlapping keywords.
   B  Cited scripts with NO committed .out -- the weakest links against the repo's own rule 4.
   C  Committed .out files containing [FAIL] -- live self-contradictions. MUST be zero.
   D  The INVERSE leak: closures banked in code but missing from STANDING. This is the class
      that hid the two-loop result, and it makes the corpus UNDERSTATE itself.
   E  Same-directory supersession by date + keyword. The two-loop pattern.

  *** EVERY HIT IS A CANDIDATE, NOT A FINDING. *** Keyword overlap cannot judge whether a newer
  script actually addresses an older claim -- only a read can. The tool is deliberately tuned to
  OVER-report: a false positive costs one read, a false negative costs a wrong standing claim.
  It computes no physics and does not attempt to.

  a0 is NOT derived, kappa = 1/2 stays FITTED, and nothing here closes or opens any physics
  door -- it only asks which doors the paperwork has mislabelled.
